you might notice that they're all divisible by 3.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:47.369
Okay, and if we take this a step further and divide them by nine, actually this is a good plug for your "casting out nines"

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.689
video. Okay. So this one has remainder six when you divide it by nine,

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.040
this is another six,

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.279
another six - starting to see a pattern here.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.880
It breaks with this one, this is remainder three.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:04.769
Okay, this is remainder three. This is remainder three and so on and so forth.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:09.959
So it turns out they all have remainder three or six when you divide them by nine. That's not an accident,

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:16.089
so as Tim explained in one of the follow-up videos it matters what the remainder is when you divide by nine.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:20.320
We know that if you get remainder four or five then actually there's no solutions at all.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:27.160
So as - the same kind of analysis shows that if you get remainder three or six, it's possible

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.660
but only just. It means the solutions for these numbers are likely to be more sparse,

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:37.869
okay, and that's what ends up making them hard to find. The modern conjecture is that every one of these numbers there'll be infinitely many solutions,

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.510
it's just they're extremely sparse

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:42.100
and so, you know

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.159
the first one can be enormous, as we've seen. We want to solve an equation

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:50.770
like x cubed plus y cubed plus z cubed equals, say 33.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.459
And we're looking for solutions where all of the numbers are below some bound.

00:02:54.459 --> 00:03:01.209
Okay, so say absolute value of x, absolute value of y, and absolute value of z are all below some bounds

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:07.989
say B, okay? So imagine B is a million or something. Now on the face of it this is a three-dimensional problem,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:14.289
okay, we've got three variables to solve for: x, y, and z and if you just went through all possibilities up to B,

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:20.589
that's somewhere on the order of B cubed numbers to check. I said you can imagine B as a million, to put things in perspective

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:26.139
your smart phone can do a million things in the blink of an eye. A million calculations like this.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:33.040
Okay, but if I say a million cubed, all right, that's in the realm of possible but very expensive

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:39.939
to do on modern computers. So this is actually very wasteful. So it's not hard to see that in this search range

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:43.329
there's loads of places where there's no point in looking,

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:48.759
okay? So if I imagine that x, y and z are all positive and large then of course

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.910
there's no way that the sum of their cubes is going to be a small number,

00:03:52.549 --> 00:03:56.709
okay? So already there's, there's big parts of the search range that you that you can cut out.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.860
If you take this idea to its logical conclusion

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:03.610
it's, it's not hard to see how to get from B cubed down to B squared.

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:06.429
This was realised pretty early on, even the very first

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.540
computer investigations that people did in the 50s on this

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:14.380
they had algorithms that were basically quadratic and would work in, in this time.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:20.049
Now that wasn't improved on for about 40 years. And then a guy called Noam Elkies at Harvard,

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.140
actually in a news group posting,

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.709
he proposed another algorithm, which is pretty amazing

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:33.160
actually. It manages to get down from B squared to basically B to the one power, so B to the 1 plus

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.730
something very small. And it's basically what enables us to go to the ranges that we are today, you know,

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:43.089
10 to the 15, 10 to the 16. It turns out that Elkies' algorithm is

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.500
most efficient when

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.950
you're looking for solutions for lots of numbers,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:54.129
as we work for a while. But you can see we're getting down to a pretty short list at this point.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:58.570
So I started to think, well maybe there are other approaches that we could look at. My approach uses more algebra.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:03.129
So what I'll do is I'll take this equation and then we'll move the z cubed over to the other side,

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:08.559
okay? So that gives us x cubed plus y cubed equals 33 minus z cubed.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.530
This is now x plus y, x squared minus xy plus y squared, equals

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:21.580
33 minus z cubed. Okay, now why have I done that? Well, we have this factor that that we know is there,

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:25.059
okay, I'm gonna give this a name. I'm gonna call it d, say for a divisor.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:30.520
All right. Now one more step, and I promise we're getting at the end of the algebra, let's divide both sides by d.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.020
Okay, so I'm gonna have on the left side just x squared minus xy

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:41.080
plus y squared. All right, this side 33 minus z cubed over d.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:44.950
Now it probably looks like I've made things a lot more complicated, right?

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.410
We started with three variables and now we've got four.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:52.239
But I want you to imagine for a moment that we know the values of z and

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:55.270
d or maybe we have some guess for them.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:00.219
And what that means is that everything on the right hand side is something I know or I can compute, on the left hand side

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:01.940
I have a quadratic equation

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.500
with two unknowns.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.910
Now as everybody knows if you've got two variables to solve for you need two equations.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:11.950
I've got one here, but there's another one that I used implicitly but didn't write down and that's this one.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:16.689
So x plus y is equal to d. All right, so now I've got two equations, two unknowns,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:21.970
we know how to solve this. Or more to the point if I guess values for z and d

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:28.420
I can tell pretty easily whether there are corresponding integer values of x and y. Okay? And this is the idea.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:33.759
We just go through all possible values for z and d and see if there's any corresponding x and y. And now that,

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:37.509
again, sounds like it should be something like b squared, right? Because there's two variables.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.159
But the key is once I've picked a value for z

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.840
the values for, for d are actually quite restricted because they have to be

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.679
divisors of this number 33 minus z cubed. And there aren't many of those.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.430
So once you've worked out z you can work out all the possible values for d

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:56.910
pretty easily, and then just try all of them. I've glossed over a few things here,

00:06:56.910 --> 00:07:00.510
so in actual practice you do it the other way around. You, you go through values of d

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.670
and then you work out z from there, but

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.190
yeah, this is the idea in a nutshell.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.870
So I use lots of computers, actually there are a few separate clusters and

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:19.799
the, the computation that found the solution was the big computer at the University of Bristol, BlueCrystal Phase 3.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:22.020
This happened way faster than I was expecting. So I

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:26.309
started this, I thought I was in for, you know, 6 months of

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:32.760
computation. My initial fear was that nothing would turn up and then I'd have to justify why I was using so many CPU cycles,

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:33.390
ok?

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:38.459
As soon as I started using the computer within a few days it found the solution. The bound I used was 10 to the 16,

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:45.420
so that's 1 followed by 16 zeroes. I had actually planned to do 10 to the 17, ok, but but one pops out

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:52.799
before I expected it. You'll see here at 9:05 in the morning on February 27th, so this was running on

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:58.440
512 CPU cores at, at the same time. One of them went through one of these values of d,

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:03.749
actually, this is the number d in the middle here. I don't know what that is, 87 trillion something, ok?

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:08.549
It turns out to be a prime number. From that it did all of this algebra, and it found the corresponding values of

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:13.049
x, y and z, and here they are. Now the computer found the solution at 9:05,

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:16.890
which is just after I drop my kids off at school, and as I was walking to the University,

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:20.939
and I noticed it around 9:30 when I got to my office.

