00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:16.959
And so if we just look at the numbers up to 100. So let me say k less than or equal to 100.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:22.810
There's 22 numbers in that range that give a remainder of 4 or 5 when you divide by 9.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:25.090
(Brady: So they're impossible? 
- So they're not possible, that's right.

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:29.440
We know the answer for those, and they're not possible. That leaves

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:36.340
seventy-eight numbers to account for. The very first computer investigation of this was done way back in 1954.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.220
That's pretty early as these things go. And they found

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.049
69. Solutions for 69 out of the 78. Well, of course, that's not the end of the story,

00:09:44.049 --> 00:09:51.400
so computer technology advanced rapidly from there and within a decade another group of mathematicians ran it on a bigger computer.

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:57.920
But they were disappointed, because they found only one more despite going about 20 times further in terms of the variables.

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:02.440
But remember they were using a quadratic time algorithm, so it's about 400 times as much work.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:06.429
So it's impressive from an engineering standpoint, yet they found only one more solution.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:10.419
So they took this as evidence that there probably aren't any more. They hedged their bets a little bit,

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:15.880
they didn't say no more solutions, but they said that not all of the remaining eight candidates would have solutions.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:21.280
That was the prevailing wisdom for a while for over twenty years. Now we think that conjecture is wrong,

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:22.580
okay?

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:27.189
Things have kind of turned around. But it wasn't until the early 90s that Heath-Brown said: well hang on,

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.309
so if there are solutions they're likely to be extremely sparse.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:30.940
In fact

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:34.989
he does some calculations and he shows that for some of these small numbers,

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:40.359
you would expect the the numbers from one solution to the next to increase by a factor of millions,

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:46.869
okay, so that means you know each time you find one you're gonna have to work a million times harder to find the next one.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:52.599
So the fact that you've, that you've expanded the search range by 20 and not found anything is not by itself

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.340
very good evidence that there's nothing more to be found. And so people looked again. From

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.050
1991

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.589
to this year,

00:11:01.589 --> 00:11:07.969
2019 we've found solutions for seven out of the eight. There's only one number left. 42 is the only one left below 100,

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.959
so we have not completely

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:16.360
disproved their conjecture from '63, but it's looking pretty good for Heath-Brown's counter-conjecture.

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:18.600
(Brady: Andrew, have you got the computer looking for 42 now?)

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:21.900
I already looked for 42 in the same range and nothing turned up,

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.360
but yeah, maybe we shouldn't stop there.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.240
(Brady: So to find 42 you've gotta go bigger?)

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:33.940
That's right, yeah. There's a definite purpose here, because this is a problem where we're not even sure, or it's not even obvious

00:11:33.949 --> 00:11:40.308
what the right conjecture should be, okay. And that's evidenced by the fact that the conjecture has changed in our lifetimes.

00:11:40.319 --> 00:11:45.259
(Brady: Does that mean, you know, a mathematician like you, instead of just picking them off one by one with a computer)

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.499
